    "body": "Fuel prices jumped 17 spots to become the trucking industry’s biggest concern in 2026, according to the American Transportation Research Institute ’s annual Top Industry Issues report . Lawsuit reform ranked second, while concerns about federal carrier safety scores climbed to third.\nWhat’s Related\nThe economy , which topped the list for three consecutive years, fell to sixth. Driver shortages and retention returned to the top 10, and artificial intelligence moved up as respondents weighed its uses against risks to jobs , security and cargo theft.\nATRI gathered responses from more than 3,000 trucking industry participants, who selected and ranked their three biggest concerns. Here are the top 10 issues, in order.\n1. Fuel Prices\nFuel costs topped the list for the first time since 2022, with 30% of respondents placing them among their three biggest concerns. ATRI noted that rising diesel prices immediately pressured fleet budgets, particularly for smaller carriers. The leading proposed response was federal action to stabilize fuel supplies and limit price increases.\n2. Lawsuit Abuse Reform\nLawsuit reform remained second overall and became the top concern among motor carrier respondents. ATRI pointed to the financial pressure from large verdicts and settlements and their impact on insurance costs. Respondents favored state limits on certain damages, changes to medical expense claims, and greater disclosure of outside funding for lawsuits.\nRELATED STORIES: Ranking the Top 50 Trucking Companies in the U.S. for 2025 | 2024 Rankings\n3. Compliance Safety Accountability\nConcerns about the Federal Motor Carrier Safety Administration’s Compliance Safety Accountability program rose three spots to their highest ranking since 2015. The program uses inspection and crash data to assess carrier safety. Respondents’ leading priority was addressing differences in state and local enforcement that can affect scores based on where fleets travel.\n4. Insurance Cost and Availability\nInsurance slipped one position but remained a major expense concern. ATRI cited research showing carrier liability premiums rose 18.2% from 2021 through 2024, while truck crash involvement declined 2.6%. Respondents favored developing a way for fleets investing in proven safety technology to receive upfront premium reductions.\n5. Driver Shortage\nDriver shortages returned to the top 10 after falling off the list in 2025. ATRI said carriers had reduced driver counts during the freight downturn, leaving concerns about hiring as demand begins to improve. Proposed responses included promoting trucking careers and creating paths for younger workers to enter the industry before becoming drivers.\n6. Economy\nThe economy fell from first to sixth, although the report described continued pressure from weak profits and rising expenses. ATRI’s latest trucking cost research put non-fuel costs at a record $1.854 per mile. Respondents wanted more research into how trucking expenses and tariffs affect equipment purchases, freight demand, and consumer prices.\n7. Artificial Intelligence in Trucking\nAI climbed three positions after entering the top 10 last year. The report highlighted uses such as driver coaching and predicting maintenance needs, alongside concerns about job displacement and increasingly sophisticated cargo theft. Respondents’ top recommendation was research into AI’s practical uses and its positive and negative effects on trucking jobs.\n8. Diesel Emissions Regulations\nDiesel emissions rules ranked eighth, reflecting concerns about truck prices, maintenance and the introduction of new emissions equipment. Respondents prioritized measuring compliance costs and allowing more flexibility in implementing stricter standards. The report also called for tracking how new emissions systems perform and hold up in everyday service.\n9. Driver Compensation\nDriver pay dropped four spots overall but remained the third-biggest concern among truck drivers and the top issue for company drivers. Respondents favored studying how different pay arrangements, including hourly, mileage and per-load pay, affect satisfaction, productivity and retention. Another priority was evaluating safety, fuel economy and retention bonuses.\n10. Driver Retention\nKeeping drivers returned to the overall top 10 for the first time since 2023. Respondents wanted a better understanding of why drivers leave and what low-turnover fleets do differently. ATRI cited research linking higher pay and shorter waits at customer facilities with lower driver turnover.",
